name: dotnet-testing-filesystem-testing-abstractions

Test file-system code with `System.IO.Abstractions`: refactor static `System.IO` usage, test file/directory operations, simulate file-system states and errors, and design testable file-processing services. Tools: `IFileSystem`, `MockFileSystem`, NSubstitute.

Static IO is slow, environment-dependent, causes disk side effects and concurrency issues, and makes error simulation difficult. `IFileSystem` exposes `IFile File`, `IDirectory Directory`, `IFileInfo FileInfo`, `IDirectoryInfo DirectoryInfo`, `IPath Path`, and `IDriveInfo DriveInfo`.

Packages:
```xml
<PackageReference Include="System.IO.Abstractions" Version="21.*" />
<PackageReference Include="System.IO.Abstractions.TestingHelpers" Version="21.*" />
```

Inject `IFileSystem` instead of calling static classes, e.g. `_fileSystem.File.ReadAllText(path)`. Register:
```csharp
services.AddSingleton<IFileSystem, FileSystem>();
services.AddScoped<ConfigService>();
```
Use `MockFileSystem` for tests, initializing files/directories with `MockFileData` and `MockDirectoryData`. Assert writes via `mockFileSystem.File.ReadAllTextAsync(...)`, and directory/file operations via `.Directory.Exists(...)` and `.File.Exists(...)`. Use a separate `MockFileSystem` per test.

For unsupported exception scenarios, use NSubstitute:
```csharp
var mockFileSystem = Substitute.For<IFileSystem>();
var mockFile = Substitute.For<IFile>();
mockFileSystem.File.Returns(mockFile);
mockFile.Exists("protected.txt").Returns(true);
mockFile.ReadAllText("protected.txt")
    .Throws(new UnauthorizedAccessException("Access denied"));
```
Also test missing files, line counts, file info, backups, copying, and writes.

Best practices: use `_fileSystem.Path.Combine(...)`; check `.File.Exists(...)`; create missing parent directories with `.Path.GetDirectoryName(...)` and `.Directory.CreateDirectory(...)`; handle `UnauthorizedAccessException`, `IOException`, and `DirectoryNotFoundException`.

**Hardcode path separators**:
// ❌ Don't do this
var path = "configs\\app.json";  // Windows only
var path = "configs/app.json";   // Unix only
// ✅ Should do this
var path = _fileSystem.Path.Combine("configs", "app.json");

**Use real file system in unit tests**:
// ❌ This is not a unit test
var realFs = new FileSystem();
// ✅ Unit tests should use MockFileSystem
var mockFs = new MockFileSystem();

**Ignore exception handling**:
// ❌ Don't assume file always exists
var content = _fileSystem.File.ReadAllText(path);
// ✅ Add existence check and exception handling
if (_fileSystem.File.Exists(path))
try { return _fileSystem.File.ReadAllText(path); }
catch (IOException) { return defaultValue; }

Only create files necessary for testing
Avoid simulating oversized files in tests

For large-file logic, use moderately sized test data, e.g. 1,000 generated lines.

Templates: `templates/configmanager-service.cs` (load/save, JSON, directory creation, backups); `templates/filemanager-service.cs` (copy/backup, file info, error handling).

Related skills: `nsubstitute-mocking`, `unit-test-fundamentals`.
Source: kevintsengtw/dotnet-testing-agent-skills (MIT), ported into dotnet-agent-harness. Based on Day 17, “File and IO Testing: Using System.IO.Abstractions to Simulate File System”: https://ithelp.ithome.com.tw/articles/10375981  
